WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:19.040
Our first speaker
is Laura Wexler.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:21.070
Laura is Professor
of American Studies,

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:23.890
Professor of Women's, Gender,
and Sexuality Studies,

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:27.340
Professor of Film and Media
Studies at Yale University.

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:29.260
She's a busy person.

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:33.850
Professor Wexler is Co-Director
of Yale Public Humanities,

00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:37.270
Founding Director of Yale's
Photographic Memory Workshop,

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:39.640
a member of the Steering
Committee of the Feminist

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:42.970
Technology Network, and
Principal Investigator

00:00:42.970 --> 00:00:45.670
of the Photogrammar Project.

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:49.300
She has served as Chair of the
Women's, Gender, and Sexuality

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:53.740
Studies Program and Co-Chair
of the Women's Faculty Forum.

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:55.990
Professor Wexler has
an undergraduate degree

00:00:55.990 --> 00:00:59.830
from Sarah Lawrence College,
and MA, M.Phil, and PhD

00:00:59.830 --> 00:01:01.720
degrees from
Columbia University.

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:03.490
Welcome Professor Wexler.

00:01:03.490 --> 00:01:06.535
Thank you very much, and
thank you to the organizers

00:01:06.535 --> 00:01:07.480
for inviting me.

00:01:10.036 --> 00:01:10.660
All right, so--

00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:11.628
Microphone.

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:25.300
OK.

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:31.930
So in the summer of 1935,
economist Rexford Tugwell,

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:33.700
who was a former
Columbia University

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:37.490
professor of economics and the
current head of the Roosevelt

00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:41.050
Administration's
Resettlement Administration,

00:01:41.050 --> 00:01:44.410
created an information
division for his agency.

00:01:44.410 --> 00:01:46.630
And he appointed to that
information division

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:51.160
another, younger Columbia
University economist,

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:55.270
Roy Stryker, to lead its
historical section that

00:01:55.270 --> 00:01:58.300
was going to be a
public relations unit.

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:00.100
The need was dire.

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:03.190
The Resettlement Administration
had replaced the Agricultural

00:02:03.190 --> 00:02:07.060
Adjustment Administration after
the former agency had created

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:10.660
an unforeseen crisis, on top of
the already critical situation

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:14.230
of American agriculture
in the Depression.

00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:17.360
By paying farmers to reduce
agricultural production,

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:19.480
the earlier government
agency had successfully

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:22.810
raised commodity prices,
which was its goal.

00:02:22.810 --> 00:02:25.870
But simultaneously, it
started pushing small farmers

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:28.390
and sharecroppers out of
their livelihood, which

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:30.460
was its unintended consequence.

00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:33.500
Family farms were being
hit by a triple whammy.

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:35.200
Modernization
brought large scale

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:38.650
industrial farming, with which
it was difficult to compete.

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:41.290
Loss of American market
share to European farms

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:44.110
that were now recovering from
the devastation of World War I

00:02:44.110 --> 00:02:46.170
brought further competition.

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:48.100
And extensive and
extended drought,

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:50.110
combined with inexpert
land management,

00:02:50.110 --> 00:02:52.720
brought the ecological
emergency of the Dust Bowl.

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:55.750
Hundreds of thousands of
formerly independent farm

00:02:55.750 --> 00:02:57.430
families were being
rapidly thrown

00:02:57.430 --> 00:03:00.460
into the migrant
transitory labor market.

00:03:00.460 --> 00:03:03.940
The Resettlement Agency,
like other New Deal agencies,

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:06.070
was set up to
mitigate the damage,

00:03:06.070 --> 00:03:08.020
and Roosevelt's
enemies in Congress

00:03:08.020 --> 00:03:11.280
were poised to stop it.

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:14.070
The main job of the Historical
Division of the Resettlement

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:16.800
Administration, therefore, was
to practice a public relations

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:19.710
policy that would be
as potent as possible.

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:21.360
The task was bureaucratic.

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:24.570
They were to supply
media with photographs

00:03:24.570 --> 00:03:27.440
that showed the success of
the government's programs.

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:30.390
Stryker hired an extraordinary
group of photographers,

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:33.660
including Dorothea Lange,
Walker Evans, Gordon Parks,

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:37.820
and sent them out on field
assignments to do this work.

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:40.600
They would work, basically
on the jobs he scripted.

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:42.850
But in the interstices,
he promised

00:03:42.850 --> 00:03:45.575
they would be able to take
a day here, a day there.

00:03:45.575 --> 00:03:47.950
That's part of why they signed
up for the government job,

00:03:47.950 --> 00:03:50.800
because you could do
that, to explore material

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:52.690
that particularly
interested them.

00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:54.490
This is where, as he
later would put it,

00:03:54.490 --> 00:03:57.370
they conspired with him to
get what history has proved

00:03:57.370 --> 00:04:00.010
to be the guts of the project.

00:04:00.010 --> 00:04:03.460
Ultimately, the greatest success
of the Historical Division

00:04:03.460 --> 00:04:06.250
of the Resettlement
Administration

00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:09.160
accomplished its goals
of introducing Americans

00:04:09.160 --> 00:04:10.590
to Americans.

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:13.690
But not only during the Great
Depression, but also long

00:04:13.690 --> 00:04:14.680
after.

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:16.690
In fact, when we
in the 21st century

00:04:16.690 --> 00:04:20.620
want to look back and see who
we once were in order to picture

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:23.080
and assess who we
are now becoming,

00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:26.290
it is very often to the
FSA photographic archive

00:04:26.290 --> 00:04:27.610
that we turn.

00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:29.950
The FSA photographs
represent the largest,

00:04:29.950 --> 00:04:32.890
indeed the only, large
scale government-sponsored

00:04:32.890 --> 00:04:35.860
documentary project in
United States history.

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:38.890
The nearly 170,000
surviving photographs

00:04:38.890 --> 00:04:42.520
of the more than 250,000
the agency produced,

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:46.090
in the 10 years
between 1935 and 1945,

00:04:46.090 --> 00:04:48.520
reside at the Library of
Congress, where access to them

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:50.650
is public and rights
of reproduction

00:04:50.650 --> 00:04:52.750
are essentially free.

00:04:52.750 --> 00:04:54.760
They are also available
for search and download

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:56.470
on the Library of
Congress website.

00:04:56.470 --> 00:04:58.690
They are widely recognized
as one of the treasures

00:04:58.690 --> 00:05:01.490
of the American people.

00:05:01.490 --> 00:05:03.710
Arguably, the FSA
photographers were

00:05:03.710 --> 00:05:05.360
more successful
than the agencies

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:07.730
they were created to support.

00:05:07.730 --> 00:05:11.210
The baseline idea was to improve
the lives of marginal farmers

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:15.080
by purchasing some marginal
land and relocating individual,

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:18.350
and often isolated, families
to better land on group

00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:20.960
farms, where economies
could be more efficient

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:23.660
and scientific expertise
could be learned, shared,

00:05:23.660 --> 00:05:25.280
and consolidated.

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:27.680
Aggressively attacked
by congressional critics

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:31.520
as collectivized agriculture,
the movers and shakers

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:32.990
of the resettlement
administration

00:05:32.990 --> 00:05:35.600
were very often on the
defensive, their budgets

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:37.190
repeatedly slashed.

00:05:37.190 --> 00:05:40.070
Instead of success, in
Tugwell's original goal

00:05:40.070 --> 00:05:43.550
of moving 650,000
people sustainably

00:05:43.550 --> 00:05:48.110
onto 100 million
acres of new land,

00:05:48.110 --> 00:05:50.150
they were only able
to move a few thousand

00:05:50.150 --> 00:05:53.840
people onto 9 million acres.

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:57.200
Instead, they were forced to
build about 75 relief camps

00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:01.730
in California, where about
75,000 destitute migrants, who

00:06:01.730 --> 00:06:05.180
were almost entirely
unwelcome in the state,

00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:08.660
were temporarily housed in clean
quarters with running water.

00:06:08.660 --> 00:06:11.120
They also built a few
greenbelt model cities

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:13.190
for a future that never came.

00:06:13.190 --> 00:06:18.290
In 1936, facing widespread
criticism Rexford Tugwell

00:06:18.290 --> 00:06:19.280
resigned.

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:22.430
And in 1937, the
Resettlement Administration

00:06:22.430 --> 00:06:25.100
was transferred to the
Department of Agriculture,

00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:28.010
and the Farm Security Act
transformed it into the Farm

00:06:28.010 --> 00:06:30.230
Security Administration.

00:06:30.230 --> 00:06:33.590
One can, and many have and
do, argue the relative value

00:06:33.590 --> 00:06:35.210
of the farming programs.

00:06:35.210 --> 00:06:37.820
One particularly stinging
criticism concerns

00:06:37.820 --> 00:06:41.630
the basic structural, and
also actively overt, racism

00:06:41.630 --> 00:06:45.200
that underlay the project,
often creating disparities

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:47.630
in who was portrayed
as the deserving poor.

00:06:47.630 --> 00:06:50.390
Whose histories of the land
are envisioned and respected,

00:06:50.390 --> 00:06:53.620
and whose were erased,
and who received relief.

00:06:53.620 --> 00:06:56.390
Key FSA programs
removed former tenants

00:06:56.390 --> 00:06:58.670
and replaced them
with FSA clients

00:06:58.670 --> 00:07:01.310
throughout the Mississippi
Delta, both black and white.

00:07:01.310 --> 00:07:03.770
But racial segregation
was accepted.

00:07:03.770 --> 00:07:06.260
Black clients at the time
were unable to challenge

00:07:06.260 --> 00:07:07.190
such policies.

00:07:07.190 --> 00:07:10.160
But it has also been argued
that the agency itself

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:13.310
created the ideological and
practical infrastructure

00:07:13.310 --> 00:07:15.890
through which the black
farmers would eventually

00:07:15.890 --> 00:07:18.860
challenge the white
supremacy of resettlement.

00:07:18.860 --> 00:07:20.720
This documentary
project, however,

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:22.460
the photographic
project is generally

00:07:22.460 --> 00:07:25.310
considered to be an
astounding success.

00:07:25.310 --> 00:07:29.810
If the more than 170,000 extant
and widely beloved photographs

00:07:29.810 --> 00:07:33.860
contain exculpatory or
contradictory evidence

00:07:33.860 --> 00:07:36.860
about the intentions and
effects of New Deal policies,

00:07:36.860 --> 00:07:39.470
it is very difficult,
however, to turn it up.

00:07:39.470 --> 00:07:44.180
The FSA archive is a very
large, very messy data set.

00:07:44.180 --> 00:07:46.970
Enter Photogrammar, a
digital research tool

00:07:46.970 --> 00:07:49.310
supported by the
NEH and the ACLS,

00:07:49.310 --> 00:07:52.460
and developed at Yale by
the Photogrammar team.

00:07:52.460 --> 00:07:55.250
The team consists of two
co-directors, former Yale

00:07:55.250 --> 00:07:58.280
graduate students Taylor
Arnold in Statistics and Lauren

00:07:58.280 --> 00:08:01.130
Tilton in American Studies,
and they're both now

00:08:01.130 --> 00:08:03.350
at the University of Richmond.

00:08:03.350 --> 00:08:05.960
Stacey Maples,
former GIS librarian

00:08:05.960 --> 00:08:08.010
at Yale, and now at
the David Ramsey Map

00:08:08.010 --> 00:08:10.130
Center at Stanford University.

00:08:10.130 --> 00:08:14.480
Kenneth Panko, former Yale
technologist, now at Yale NUS.

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:17.360
Peter Leonard, Yale's first
digital humanities librarian

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:20.300
and head of the DH Lab in
Sterling Memorial Library.

00:08:20.300 --> 00:08:23.540
Trip Kirkpatrick, technologist
in Yale's Teaching and Learning

00:08:23.540 --> 00:08:24.560
Laboratory.

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:27.400
And myself, after
six years, profoundly

00:08:27.400 --> 00:08:34.220
a digitally-enhanced humanist,
I am the principal investigator

00:08:34.220 --> 00:08:36.059
of the Photogrammar Project.

00:08:36.059 --> 00:08:41.120
One intervention that
we immediately made

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:47.840
was to create a web-based,
many-layered interactive

00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:52.820
geospatial map to render this
archive fluently searchable.

00:08:52.820 --> 00:08:58.040
And you see in this slide
the mapping that resulted.

00:08:58.040 --> 00:09:01.070
And when we did this,
absolutely immediately we

00:09:01.070 --> 00:09:05.900
overturned the first
stereotypical truism

00:09:05.900 --> 00:09:08.480
that scholars have,
since the 1930s,

00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:10.610
been repeating about
this project, which

00:09:10.610 --> 00:09:14.510
is that it is about
Appalachia and the Dust Bowl.

00:09:14.510 --> 00:09:18.350
The minute that you map the
photographs, what you find

00:09:18.350 --> 00:09:21.110
is that it's a project
of national scope.

00:09:21.110 --> 00:09:27.380
And that it covers not
only from Maine to Florida,

00:09:27.380 --> 00:09:31.220
there's even sites
offshore, but also

00:09:31.220 --> 00:09:36.170
the old industrial cities,
as well as the Heartland

00:09:36.170 --> 00:09:38.750
and the farmland of the country.

00:09:38.750 --> 00:09:43.010
And why this is and why
it keeps getting repeated,

00:09:43.010 --> 00:09:46.310
that really it's about
Appalachian and the Okies

00:09:46.310 --> 00:09:48.980
moving west to
California, is something

00:09:48.980 --> 00:09:52.520
that I believe that mapping
and studying the FSA

00:09:52.520 --> 00:09:56.150
archive as a big messy
data set will eventually

00:09:56.150 --> 00:09:59.430
allow us to learn about.

00:09:59.430 --> 00:10:02.040
So let me just give
you a quick torrent,

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:04.620
slides of the web site.

00:10:04.620 --> 00:10:07.360
You will recognize many
of these photographs.

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:10.810
They are not only our
cultural heritage,

00:10:10.810 --> 00:10:15.660
but our cultural imaginary of
who we were during the Great

00:10:15.660 --> 00:10:16.200
Depression.

00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:18.750
These are Walker
Evans' photographs

00:10:18.750 --> 00:10:22.170
of the sharecroppers that
were published in the book

00:10:22.170 --> 00:10:24.720
Let Us Now Praise Famous
Men that he coauthored

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:27.000
with James Agee.

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:35.430
This is the office of the
women in the back offices, who

00:10:35.430 --> 00:10:40.860
were categorizing and tagging
the hundreds of thousands

00:10:40.860 --> 00:10:44.040
of photographs as they
came in, under the auspices

00:10:44.040 --> 00:10:48.330
of a brilliant librarian named
Paul Vanderbilt. This was

00:10:48.330 --> 00:10:52.170
Vanderbilt's first
effort at tagging,

00:10:52.170 --> 00:10:58.890
actually creating visual
categories for photographs.

00:10:58.890 --> 00:11:01.740
He went on to head the
Wisconsin Historical Society

00:11:01.740 --> 00:11:03.480
and do incredible work there.

00:11:03.480 --> 00:11:07.710
But these are the labor force
behind what Vanderbilt was

00:11:07.710 --> 00:11:10.950
doing, and they are anonymous.

00:11:10.950 --> 00:11:13.860
The FSA files exist
in red file cabinets

00:11:13.860 --> 00:11:18.210
in the Library of Congress
now as analog objects,

00:11:18.210 --> 00:11:19.650
material objects.

00:11:19.650 --> 00:11:22.950
And they also exist
online in the Library

00:11:22.950 --> 00:11:24.510
of Congress website.

00:11:24.510 --> 00:11:28.590
But both of these are very,
very difficult to search.

00:11:28.590 --> 00:11:31.800
In 2001, I was part of a small
group of scholars brought

00:11:31.800 --> 00:11:34.650
to the Library of Congress
to search physically

00:11:34.650 --> 00:11:40.920
through these drawers and
find interesting information

00:11:40.920 --> 00:11:44.550
about the FSA photographs
that we didn't already know.

00:11:44.550 --> 00:11:51.090
And out of the, I think
probably eight or nine of us,

00:11:51.090 --> 00:11:54.870
very little new interesting
information resulted from that.

00:11:54.870 --> 00:12:01.260
Because even in a week of
looking, it's too unwieldy,

00:12:01.260 --> 00:12:03.900
it's too difficult, there
are too many factors,

00:12:03.900 --> 00:12:07.740
there's too much information
to search that way.

00:12:07.740 --> 00:12:10.680
And this is one of the reasons
that the Photogrammar Project

00:12:10.680 --> 00:12:12.510
got started.

00:12:12.510 --> 00:12:16.980
So here's the map.

00:12:16.980 --> 00:12:28.440
And we also took 10 of the
primary, most famous, most

00:12:28.440 --> 00:12:31.140
well-known photographers,
and gave them colors,

00:12:31.140 --> 00:12:36.180
and tracked their trail
across the country.

00:12:36.180 --> 00:12:43.590
And we interleaved this
with a 1937 highway map.

00:12:43.590 --> 00:12:50.130
Because if you're looking
for mystified or occluded

00:12:50.130 --> 00:12:55.650
political choices in where
the FSA was doing its work,

00:12:55.650 --> 00:12:59.400
one of the ways to
try to figure that out

00:12:59.400 --> 00:13:04.110
is to see whether the people
doing field work were following

00:13:04.110 --> 00:13:10.770
the main transportation routes,
were going on the back roads,

00:13:10.770 --> 00:13:15.270
were relating to minority
communities or not,

00:13:15.270 --> 00:13:17.790
it's the roadmap that
can tell you that.

00:13:17.790 --> 00:13:20.100
We also discovered this.

00:13:20.100 --> 00:13:22.680
The blue dots that
we first thought

00:13:22.680 --> 00:13:27.420
were Route 66 going west,
it's actually a train.

00:13:27.420 --> 00:13:28.995
It's not Route 66 at all.

00:13:32.590 --> 00:13:37.110
So that's the trip going west.

00:13:37.110 --> 00:13:39.480
We really threaded
the needle, in terms

00:13:39.480 --> 00:13:42.390
of a digital humanities project,
with the Photogrammar Project

00:13:42.390 --> 00:13:45.480
because this is open
access, open data that's

00:13:45.480 --> 00:13:48.120
owned by the people
of the United States.

00:13:48.120 --> 00:13:52.030
And so we were able, with
the tremendous, tremendous,

00:13:52.030 --> 00:13:55.430
wonderful willing cooperation
of the Library of Congress,

00:13:55.430 --> 00:14:03.360
we were able to work with
the beautifully-digitized

00:14:03.360 --> 00:14:05.730
photographs that
they, twice, did

00:14:05.730 --> 00:14:09.480
in two different generations
of digital capacity

00:14:09.480 --> 00:14:11.220
and put online.

00:14:11.220 --> 00:14:18.210
And with the metadata that they
also, as wonderful librarians,

00:14:18.210 --> 00:14:21.780
kept attached, this is
public open information.

00:14:21.780 --> 00:14:25.320
When we've presented this
project in other fora,

00:14:25.320 --> 00:14:30.720
other groups of people have
said, well, could we do, could

00:14:30.720 --> 00:14:32.260
we do Photogrammar on our--

00:14:32.260 --> 00:14:37.110
The US Holocaust
Memorial Museum wanted

00:14:37.110 --> 00:14:42.900
to do a Photogrammar kind of
map with letters and photographs

00:14:42.900 --> 00:14:45.630
and information that
people were sending them.

00:14:45.630 --> 00:14:49.710
But it turns out that
there are restrictions

00:14:49.710 --> 00:14:50.700
on the information.

00:14:50.700 --> 00:14:52.140
It's a proprietary archive.

00:14:54.930 --> 00:14:57.670
Again and again and
again, people contact us.

00:14:57.670 --> 00:15:01.830
We say all of our coding, all of
our information, is on GitHub.

00:15:01.830 --> 00:15:02.910
It's all free for you.

00:15:02.910 --> 00:15:04.510
Talk to us, we're
happy to help you.

00:15:04.510 --> 00:15:10.560
And they cannot do this
work because the foresight

00:15:10.560 --> 00:15:15.390
or happenchance or whatever it
is of the government, in 1936,

00:15:15.390 --> 00:15:17.195
made this a public archive.

00:15:24.980 --> 00:15:30.710
And so you see something
we've circled here

00:15:30.710 --> 00:15:34.270
on the attached metadata
called lot number.

00:15:34.270 --> 00:15:37.370
And that has to do with
the original coding system

00:15:37.370 --> 00:15:41.160
then Vanderbilt, in that
room of workers, was doing.

00:15:41.160 --> 00:15:51.110
And we also got a hold of
Vanderbilt's key handbook

00:15:51.110 --> 00:15:54.200
and we coordinated
his categories

00:15:54.200 --> 00:15:56.390
with these
photographs, so that we

00:15:56.390 --> 00:15:59.210
can see how he
and the government

00:15:59.210 --> 00:16:02.510
and the general
media were thinking

00:16:02.510 --> 00:16:06.380
of this portrait of
Americans at the time.

00:16:06.380 --> 00:16:08.270
One of the categories
we actually love

00:16:08.270 --> 00:16:12.071
is "people as such," without
emphasis on their activity.

00:16:12.071 --> 00:16:13.695
That is actually one
of his categories.

00:16:13.695 --> 00:16:15.440
"People as such."

00:16:15.440 --> 00:16:19.760
I think this may be one
of the "people as such."

00:16:19.760 --> 00:16:25.730
But what you see is that,
because these photographs are

00:16:25.730 --> 00:16:30.020
publicly owned, scholars
of the United States

00:16:30.020 --> 00:16:35.270
have gone to this archive to
get images for their books

00:16:35.270 --> 00:16:37.640
and articles without
much expense,

00:16:37.640 --> 00:16:39.950
and you can always
get permission.

00:16:39.950 --> 00:16:42.482
And what that means
is there's been,

00:16:42.482 --> 00:16:43.940
I don't know what
a scientist would

00:16:43.940 --> 00:16:48.490
call it, but a high replication
effect of a limited number

00:16:48.490 --> 00:16:51.140
of photographs.

00:16:51.140 --> 00:16:54.980
And so we actually
only a small amount

00:16:54.980 --> 00:16:57.740
of what the images
are in this archive.

00:16:57.740 --> 00:17:00.950
And every single time
I open up Photogrammar,

00:17:00.950 --> 00:17:03.080
I find new images.

00:17:07.040 --> 00:17:09.560
One of the innovations
of Photogrammar, so

00:17:09.560 --> 00:17:13.130
what Stryker and the team
were doing in the 1930s

00:17:13.130 --> 00:17:15.319
was turning data into images.

00:17:15.319 --> 00:17:17.089
The government had
data [? where ?]

00:17:17.089 --> 00:17:20.540
the agencies working, and he
was turning it into images

00:17:20.540 --> 00:17:24.589
so that Americans could see and
understand who one another are.

00:17:24.589 --> 00:17:26.359
What we have done
with those images

00:17:26.359 --> 00:17:29.360
is to turn them back
into data, which gives us

00:17:29.360 --> 00:17:35.720
now another way of searching,
combining, inquiring,

00:17:35.720 --> 00:17:41.090
and understanding the nature
of this photographic system,

00:17:41.090 --> 00:17:44.780
and how it applies
to our political past

00:17:44.780 --> 00:17:47.090
and our current situation.

00:17:47.090 --> 00:17:51.710
This is a treemap we
made Vanderbilt's 1942

00:17:51.710 --> 00:17:52.790
classification system.

00:17:52.790 --> 00:17:54.610
You can see "people as such."

00:17:54.610 --> 00:17:59.540
You drill down into
it in three segments,

00:17:59.540 --> 00:18:02.330
and you see what
they're looking for.

00:18:02.330 --> 00:18:06.860
Horse shows, circus, dance,
orchestras, group singing.

00:18:06.860 --> 00:18:08.690
This is the way
they are thinking

00:18:08.690 --> 00:18:10.370
of a portrait of the country.

00:18:10.370 --> 00:18:11.840
Is it our portrait
of the country?

00:18:11.840 --> 00:18:13.010
No.

00:18:13.010 --> 00:18:14.750
What's interesting
is the difference

00:18:14.750 --> 00:18:16.610
between that portrait
of the country

00:18:16.610 --> 00:18:18.050
and our portrait of the country.

00:18:20.570 --> 00:18:23.390
I'm going to go very quickly
through and hope to show you

00:18:23.390 --> 00:18:25.350
one more thing.

00:18:25.350 --> 00:18:26.540
There are color ones.

00:18:26.540 --> 00:18:29.070
Hardly anybody knew that.

00:18:29.070 --> 00:18:32.270
We have been using
facial recognition

00:18:32.270 --> 00:18:35.400
to categorize the photographs.

00:18:35.400 --> 00:18:40.130
This is not very theoretically
well-substantiated.

00:18:40.130 --> 00:18:48.610
We are looking for
morphological features

00:18:48.610 --> 00:18:51.530
and trying to see what kind
of, we're just experimenting

00:18:51.530 --> 00:18:55.591
with the images and trying
to see what kind of pictures

00:18:55.591 --> 00:18:56.090
we get.

00:18:56.090 --> 00:18:58.820
And can we actually find
more African-Americans,

00:18:58.820 --> 00:19:01.680
visible African-Americans,
this way.

00:19:01.680 --> 00:19:04.430
And then finally,
I want to tell you

00:19:04.430 --> 00:19:08.420
a story that would
never have happened

00:19:08.420 --> 00:19:13.100
if it wasn't this kind of a
group, this kind of a totally

00:19:13.100 --> 00:19:15.470
inter-disciplinary,
cross-disciplinary group

00:19:15.470 --> 00:19:19.280
with technologists, a
digital humanities librarian.

00:19:19.280 --> 00:19:21.650
Me, I'm a historian
of photography.

00:19:21.650 --> 00:19:24.950
Someone who had worked in
museums, a statistician, a GIS

00:19:24.950 --> 00:19:25.490
expert.

00:19:25.490 --> 00:19:27.080
This is our team.

00:19:27.080 --> 00:19:29.480
We were in Washington
and talking

00:19:29.480 --> 00:19:33.020
to Beverly Bannon,
who's the chief

00:19:33.020 --> 00:19:34.872
[? curator ?] of this archive.

00:19:34.872 --> 00:19:36.580
We were around a table
and Taylor Arnold,

00:19:36.580 --> 00:19:40.430
this statistics grad
student, he finished his PhD,

00:19:40.430 --> 00:19:42.760
but at the time, was
sitting in a corner

00:19:42.760 --> 00:19:49.520
and he was looking at this
call number lalalala-M4.

00:19:49.520 --> 00:19:52.190
And he says, what
does that mean, M4?

00:19:52.190 --> 00:19:55.880
And the librarian
says, we don't know.

00:19:55.880 --> 00:19:57.950
We don't know,
but best practices

00:19:57.950 --> 00:20:03.020
are to keep the MARC record,
keep the metadata, intact.

00:20:03.020 --> 00:20:05.930
So in all these years,
the librarians in Congress

00:20:05.930 --> 00:20:06.620
did that.

00:20:06.620 --> 00:20:08.210
What is this M number?

00:20:08.210 --> 00:20:13.610
And after thinking about
it for a long time,

00:20:13.610 --> 00:20:21.040
Taylor and Lauren figured
out is that M number

00:20:21.040 --> 00:20:22.190
has to do with this.

00:20:22.190 --> 00:20:25.990
The government
photographers were supplied

00:20:25.990 --> 00:20:27.130
by government supplies.

00:20:27.130 --> 00:20:31.135
And it was cheaper to give
them a large, industrial roll

00:20:31.135 --> 00:20:33.910
of film than the film that,
those of you who would even

00:20:33.910 --> 00:20:36.190
remember film will
remember, used to come

00:20:36.190 --> 00:20:39.040
in 24- and 36-exposure rolls.

00:20:39.040 --> 00:20:42.970
So they had large
rolls, and they

00:20:42.970 --> 00:20:46.010
were constrained to
go out in the field

00:20:46.010 --> 00:20:48.970
and shoot pictures and
send a rolls of film

00:20:48.970 --> 00:20:55.270
back to Washington, where a
lab developed and printed them.

00:20:55.270 --> 00:20:58.090
And then there's a
process of choosing.

00:20:58.090 --> 00:21:02.830
So the lab did this, and
it caught the rolls of film

00:21:02.830 --> 00:21:08.050
into about six- or
eight-frame segments.

00:21:08.050 --> 00:21:10.720
When they were done,
they threw the segments

00:21:10.720 --> 00:21:12.370
into a metaphorical pot.

00:21:12.370 --> 00:21:14.410
They were not
interested in keeping

00:21:14.410 --> 00:21:15.550
the segments aggregated.

00:21:15.550 --> 00:21:17.620
They were looking
for individual images

00:21:17.620 --> 00:21:21.280
to substantiate reports
in government documents.

00:21:21.280 --> 00:21:23.020
So they threw these, OK.

00:21:23.020 --> 00:21:25.570
So what Taylor figured
out is that the M number

00:21:25.570 --> 00:21:30.700
had to do with which frame
it is on the negative strip.

00:21:30.700 --> 00:21:33.160
And when he was able
to write a program

00:21:33.160 --> 00:21:37.690
that could re-unify
the negative strips.

00:21:37.690 --> 00:21:42.730
So here's an example, John
Vachon in July of 1941.

00:21:42.730 --> 00:21:46.060
He's in Wisconsin and
he's photographing cheese.

00:21:46.060 --> 00:21:50.140
Cheese, cheese, cheese,
cheese, cheese, cheese, cheese.

00:21:50.140 --> 00:21:51.990
Then there's a blank.

00:21:51.990 --> 00:21:53.710
There's a blank frame.

00:21:53.710 --> 00:21:57.040
And that blank frame represents,
actually, his crossing.

00:21:57.040 --> 00:22:01.180
The next frame is to Chicago,
where that next picture

00:22:01.180 --> 00:22:05.740
is as a down-and-out
man sitting,

00:22:05.740 --> 00:22:07.970
I guess it's on a fire hydrant.

00:22:07.970 --> 00:22:10.450
And then the train of
thought, the shooting,

00:22:10.450 --> 00:22:13.130
goes to photographing that man.

00:22:13.130 --> 00:22:15.370
Now, it's absolutely
extraordinary.

00:22:15.370 --> 00:22:18.790
We've been able to reunify
a certain a number of these.

00:22:18.790 --> 00:22:20.980
And we now have a
grant, Taylor and Lauren

00:22:20.980 --> 00:22:25.300
are working on this in Richmond,
to reunify many, many more.

00:22:25.300 --> 00:22:31.850
But what you have here,
or now the historian,

00:22:31.850 --> 00:22:33.110
is this question.

00:22:33.110 --> 00:22:36.350
Why is the jump
from stop cheese,

00:22:36.350 --> 00:22:40.880
then you can figure I'm tired of
cheese, to this man in Chicago.

00:22:40.880 --> 00:22:44.630
And the question for a
Depression historian,

00:22:44.630 --> 00:22:46.940
the question that
arises here is,

00:22:46.940 --> 00:22:52.700
is John Vachon thinking we make
so much cheese in this country,

00:22:52.700 --> 00:22:55.910
Wisconsin is not very
far from Chicago,

00:22:55.910 --> 00:23:01.400
what is the reason we cannot
get the cheese to the people who

00:23:01.400 --> 00:23:02.630
are hungry?

00:23:02.630 --> 00:23:08.660
Cheese now comes in aid baskets.

00:23:08.660 --> 00:23:12.890
And I wonder whether this
is a prehistory of that.

00:23:12.890 --> 00:23:15.050
But the question is
a question that goes

00:23:15.050 --> 00:23:17.480
to the heart of the New Deal.

00:23:17.480 --> 00:23:21.470
Do we need a different kind
of production and distribution

00:23:21.470 --> 00:23:26.910
system in order to
get what is needed

00:23:26.910 --> 00:23:28.706
to the people who need it?

00:23:31.590 --> 00:23:32.580
That's the question.

00:23:32.580 --> 00:23:33.420
So, all right.

00:23:33.420 --> 00:23:36.390
So Roy Stryker's
brilliant photographers

00:23:36.390 --> 00:23:38.280
transformed data
into photographs.

00:23:38.280 --> 00:23:40.110
I've said that the
brilliant Photogrammar

00:23:40.110 --> 00:23:44.400
team has transformed these
photographs back into data.

00:23:44.400 --> 00:23:48.450
The enduring question is, from
a digitally-enhanced humanist

00:23:48.450 --> 00:23:51.330
point of view, cui bono?

00:23:51.330 --> 00:23:54.960
Who benefits from this
knowledge, and how?

00:23:54.960 --> 00:23:56.790
Thank you.

